  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 320 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nCool Canadian high pressure will drift southeast from south \ncentral Canada into the upper Mississippi Valley Sunday into \nMonday. This will allow cold air to persist across the forecast \narea Sunday and Monday. Highs both days will struggle to reach \nthe lower 40s which is 5 to 10 degrees below normal for this \ntime of year. On Monday, an upper level trough of low pressure \nwill cross the intermountain west emerging onto the high plains \nTuesday morning. The latest EC and GFS deterministic solns \ndevelop some mid level frontogenesis across the area Monday \nnight into Tuesday morning, developing some light precipitation \nacross the forecast area. Temperatures Monday night into Tuesday\nmorning are cold enough to support snow. As for snow amounts, \nthe latest NBM ensemble forecast has a 25 percent chance for 1 \ninch of 24 hour snow accumulation ending at 6 PM CT on Tuesday. \nATTM the best chances for snow are over the western Sandhills \nand portions of SW Nebraska. When the snow accumulation is \nlowered to 0.5 inches, there is up to a 40 percent chance of \nreaching 0.5 inches. The latest WPC forecast has around a 30 \npercent chance of snow overnight Monday into Tuesday morning. \nBased on the latest NBM ensembles and the deterministic GFS and \nEC solns, believe this forecast appears on track. The upper \nlevel trough will track southeast into the southern plains \nTuesday night into Wednesday. Ridging will then build in behind \nthe exiting trough Wednesday night into Thursday. Warmer air \nappears to push into the area Thursday and the latest MEX and \nECX statistical guidance is indicative of highs in the 50s. \nHowever, the latest NBM ensembles keep highs in the 40s for \nWednesday and Thursday. This is echoed by the latest GFS and EC \nensemble forecast which favors 40s vs. 50s for highs those days.\nThat being said, the latest deterministic solns and statistical\nguidance seems to be trending warmer and highs may need to be \nadjusted upward Wednesday and Thursday with subsequent \nforecasts.",
